WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:01.470
JAMES WATTERS:
What's happening now

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.636
is that software is starting
to take on its own life

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.820
and building completely
new kinds of processes that

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.860
never existed in paper before.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.920
And those are what's really
differentiating businesses.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.200
And so the lifecycle of those,
and the change rate of those,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.810
changes as fast as
people can consume them.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.540
And this is a big change, from
a once-a-year deployment to we

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.390
have enterprises doing
20 deployments a week.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.270
And so that clock speed
change and that style

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.911
of process change that's
happening is really remarkable.

00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:32.369
So this is where you hear this
word digital transformation,

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.350
which I don't open
with, but that's

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.210
really what all of these
changes lead up to.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.160
That change is, I think, at the
heart of sort of the Pivotal

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.430
and Google partnership,
which is we're really

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.250
bringing the ability to
go compete with software

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.230
and be great at
software, and do it

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.470
in a scalable,
data-integrated way to all

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.480
these large enterprises.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.640
SAM RAMJI: We used to have
scarce, giant resources,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.914
like mainframes, and the few
heroes who could use them.

00:00:56.914 --> 00:00:58.830
But now we've got an
abundance of computation.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.210
It's basically free.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.760
Now, from 1992 to 2012,
the cost of computation

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.820
dropped by a factor of 3,700.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.020
The cost of storage dropped
by a factor of 18,900.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.590
So you think about the
absolute, all-out abundance

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.780
of computational infrastructure.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.817
Well then, you've
got a new scarcity.

00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:22.650
The scarcity is actually
the developers, not

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.719
the infrastructure.

00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:26.010
So what they want, what they
want to deploy, the things

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.710
they can imagine, actually
have a much bigger impact

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.800
on your business than the
cost of the infrastructure.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.300
We focused on
developer productivity

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.675
from another point
of view, which

00:01:34.675 --> 00:01:39.150
is, how can you build
billion-user experiences that

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.450
can rapidly iterate based
on what users want, and not

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.920
also burden those developers
with understanding scale

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.140
and reliability?

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.960
So we've taken our
internal competencies,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.929
which we call technical
infrastructure-- it's

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:51.720
the opposite of IT.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:52.360
Get it?

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.320
It's TI.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.800
About 11,000 engineers
who do nothing but support

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.920
the other 40,000 engineers who
support Gmail, search, ads,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:00.420
right?

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.044
We have seven different
businesses that

00:02:02.044 --> 00:02:04.110
have over a billion users each.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.319
So that partnership is
actually how Google runs.

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:07.860
It's all about
developer productivity

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:08.940
for our developers.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.919
The partnership with
Pivotal is about bringing

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:13.614
the best of what we know about
what that infrastructure is

00:02:13.614 --> 00:02:15.030
to the best of
what Pivotal knows,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.610
which is what are the runtime
platforms and the tooling,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.590
like Spring, that
developers really love,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.210
because it just gets
out of their way

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.110
and they get things done.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.070
If your software practice
is not incredibly agile,

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.070
your business process
is stuck in concrete,

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.720
and you wont evolve.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.940
It's very satisfying to
sit here a year later

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.460
and say Kubernetes and Cloud
Foundry are coming together

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.910
very pragmatically,
in some obvious ways,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.310
but also some surprising
and very beautiful ways.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.840
The de-risking of
open source helps

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.250
you reduce migration cost.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.800
You can bet on Kubernetes,
you can bet on Cloud Foundry,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.320
and know that there are vibrant
multi-vendor communities.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.930
Everybody is worried about
making the wrong choice.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.090
So how are they
de-risking their decisions

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.830
so that they can
make a decision now?

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.360
And everybody is worried
about developer productivity.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.310
We've got a shared
infrastructure, the shared

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.450
management layer in BOSH.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.000
We've got a shared
service broker,

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.750
so you can get to all
of the same services

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.166
like data services or analytics
services, machine learning

00:03:09.166 --> 00:03:11.130
services, whatever you
want, wherever you are.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:12.540
So just make it work.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.690
So that joint engineering,
customer obsession, and this

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.800
focus on eliminating this risk
of this or that by making it an

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.100
and is a really big deal.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.070
And if you watch where
the puck is going,

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.330
there's a lot more coming.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.520
The truth is, value's
only found in the market

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.691
by customer interaction.

00:03:27.691 --> 00:03:29.190
What you start with
is always wrong,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:30.606
and then you have
to incrementally

00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:31.800
get it less wrong.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.780
So its all about cycle
time and velocity.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.220
If I can iterate
my product based

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.860
on customer feedback
in one week,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.820
and somebody else
takes a month, then I

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.660
can get 52 cycles in a
year, and they only get 12.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.480
My software will be so much
smarter because its compounding

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.630
interest.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.754
So that's what I mean when I
say you have to bet on velocity.

00:03:49.754 --> 00:03:51.420
So when you look at
the Pivotal sprints,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.253
when you look at the
two-week release cycles

00:03:53.253 --> 00:03:56.460
that you get in Cloud Foundry,
when you look at the 9,000,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.430
10,000 times that we release
software per day in Google,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.890
like, we're companies
who've been practicing

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.510
velocity for a long time.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.760
I authentically believe we've
got a great opportunity,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.560
and there's no two
better companies

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.930
to go and do this for the
world than Pivotal and Google.

